  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 201 AM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nMonday and Tuesday...upper ridging will settle over the Red River \nValley of the south with broad southwesterly flow crossing the spine \nof the Rockies. Longwave troughing will lift north and east into the \nnorthern High Plains through the day Monday with a deepening low \npressure aloft forming as the disturbance crosses into southern \nCanada. A trailing cold front will settle south into the area late \nMonday and stalling through the day on Tuesday. This will serve as \nthe focus for a couple days of increased rain and thunderstorm \npotential with model blend PoPs painting definites (> 74%) Monday \nevening and only slight decreases heading into early Tuesday. These \ninherited PoPs seem a bit aggressive given fairly lackluster NBM QPF \nprobabilities where exceeding 0.25\" is limited to around 20-30%. Do \nanticipate some ebb and flow with this period of the mid-range \nforecast as precise location of a frontal boundary at this time \nscale is far from certain. Similarly, the placement of the frontal \nboundary and timing of rain/thunderstorm development could play a \nlarge role in determining daytime highs on Monday. Overall the NBM \nshows little spread in Monday and Tuesday MaxT values with inner-\nquartile spreads of only 4F and 5F respectively for both North \nPlatte and Valentine.\n\nWednesday and beyond...upper ridging will build west and deep \ntroughing tracks towards the Hudson Bay. The result will be largely \nzonal flow arriving towards the middle of the next week. At the \nsurface, the aforementioned frontal boundary will settle south with \nreinforcing high pressure returning to much of the central and \nnorthern Plains. Moisture will largely be scoured out and large \nscale lift bypassing the area. This suggests more limited \nprecipitation chances beyond Day 5. While the core of high pressure \naloft will be just south of the area, general westerly flow with \npositive temperature anomalies nearby will maintain near to slightly \nabove normal temperatures through the end of the forecast period \nnext weekend. The latest Climate Prediction Center outlooks general \nfavors this idea as well with both 6-10 Day and 8-14 Day products \nshowing considerable warm leans.",
